# The application Kconfig of this entry (phase8-W17): Zephyr's, plus the
# island's trace-window options and the assert record.
rsource "../safety_island_tracing/Kconfig.island_trace"

config ISLAND_ASSERT_RECORD
	bool "Record a failing assert's file and line for the SWD readout"
	depends on ASSERT
	default y
	help
	  phase9-C4. Overrides assert_post_action() (src/assert_record.c): the
	  first failing __ASSERT writes its file, line and uptime into
	  island_assert_record, then panics; tools/board/readout.py writes
	  assert.txt from it. The console is not wired on the board.

config ISLAND_DEBUG_FORCE_ASSERT_MS
	int "Fail an __ASSERT this many ms after boot (test only)"
	depends on ISLAND_ASSERT_RECORD
	default 0
	help
	  phase9-C4's gate: 0 (the default) is off. Never set in a committed
	  conf.

config ISLAND_DEBUG_FORCE_ASSERT_ISR
	bool "Fire the forced assert from a timer ISR instead of the work queue"
	depends on ISLAND_DEBUG_FORCE_ASSERT_MS > 0

config ISLAND_DEBUG_FORCE_ASSERT_LOCKED
	bool "Fire the forced assert under irq_lock() with the shell's lock held"
	depends on ISLAND_DEBUG_FORCE_ASSERT_MS > 0 && !ISLAND_DEBUG_FORCE_ASSERT_ISR
	depends on SHELL_BACKEND_SERIAL
	help
	  The condition of the I16 assert (dma_mcux_edma.c under irq_lock,
	  nano-ros phase-474) with the shell busy: an immediate-mode print of
	  the assert has to pend on the shell's lock with interrupts locked.

config ISLAND_LED
	bool "Show the MRM handler's state on the board's LEDs"
	depends on GPIO
	help
	  The user RGB LED (aliases led0..led2) shows the handler's MRM state
	  and CAN LED0 is a 1 Hz heartbeat, both set from the handler's own
	  10 Hz tick (src/island_led.c; docs/design/carla-island-demo.md,
	  section 3). island_led_record keeps the shown pattern and its last
	  transitions for the SWD readout (tools/board/led_readout.py).
	  Off by default: the gate image does not carry it.

config ISLAND_BUTTONS
	bool "Record the board's SW1/SW2 presses for the demo's acts"
	depends on GPIO
	help
	  SW1 (alias sw0) is the driver's takeover, SW2 (alias sw1) the
	  encore's HPC loss. Read from the MRM handler's own 10 Hz tick by the
	  GPIO driver's port read (no CTF record, no ISR), debounced over two
	  ticks, and recorded with the handler's state in island_button_record
	  (src/island_buttons.c); the act on the host reads the record over
	  SWD and does the rest (docs/design/carla-island-demo.md, section
	  3.4, unit C6). A demo affordance, not a safety function: the island
	  does not act on a press. Off by default: the gate image does not
	  carry it.

config ISLAND_LOCK_PROBE
	bool "Price the executor lock on target (nano-ros phase-509 I4)"
	depends on THREAD_ANALYZER && INIT_STACKS && THREAD_NAME
	help
	  A measurement image only (src/island_lock_probe.c): the k_mutex
	  lock cost at boot, every hold of the executor's lock (wrapping
	  nros_cpp_executor_set_lock) for L_max, and each thread's stack
	  high-water, into island_lock_probe_record for an SWD readout
	  (tools/board/lock_probe.py). Off in the gate image.

config ISLAND_LOCK_PROBE_STEADY_MS
	int "Uptime from which a hold counts as steady state (ms)"
	depends on ISLAND_LOCK_PROBE
	default 8000
	help
	  Holds and waits before this uptime (registration, the levels'
	  bring-up, the session's join) are kept apart from the steady-state
	  maxima.

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR
	bool "Supervised deadlines: a timer and a thread outside the executor (phase11-S4)"
	depends on GPIO
	help
	  The rung that does not need the executor (docs/roadmap/
	  phase-11-supervised-budgets.md, S4; src/island_supervisor.c). A
	  k_timer is re-armed to the earliest due time of the supervised
	  routes; its expiry (ISR) wakes a reaction thread above the spin
	  thread's priority, which records a trace marker and drives the
	  safe-state output (CAN LED1, PTE5, standing in for a brake line). The
	  thread never touches the executor or the zenoh session, neither of
	  which is thread-safe. Until nano-ros carries a deadline-armed route
	  monitor (phase-11 S3), the MRM handler arms the route itself: each
	  take of the availability sets the due of the hpc_loss reaction
	  (take + max_age + call_mrm), and the call_mrm EXIT of a tick that
	  saw the loss clears it. The output latches until reset.

if ISLAND_SUPERVISOR

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_CALL_MRM_MS
	int "call_mrm's supervised budget (ms)"
	default 149
	help
	  The contract's mrm_handler/call_mrm max_latency (149 ms on the
	  board: tick 118 + work 31). The handler adds it to the
	  availability's max_age (its own timeout_operation_mode_availability
	  parameter) for the hpc_loss reaction's due from the last take.
	  tools/eval/analysis.py reads the due back from the trace and
	  compares it with the contract's.

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_STACK_SIZE
	int "Reaction thread stack (bytes)"
	default 6144
	help
	  The reaction thread runs the direct emergency path (phase11-S6):
	  the handler's and the emergency operator's publishes, CDR
	  serialisation and the zenoh-pico put included. 1024 sufficed while it
	  only drove the output.
	  4096 was 99 % used: the lock-probe image read 4,048 of 4,096 B on
	  the 300 ms overrun encore (supervisor latched; run mt-lp-ov,
	  2026-10-25), 240 B when the supervisor is not driven.

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_DIRECT_PERIOD_MS
	int "Direct emergency path period (ms)"
	default 33
	help
	  Once the output is latched, how often the reaction thread repeats
	  each component's direct emergency publish until that component's
	  executor path takes over (island_supervisor_handover): the
	  emergency operator's own update_rate 30.

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_COMFORTABLE_MS
	int "The comfortable stop's standstill deadline (ms)"
	default 10940
	help
	  Route 2 (phase11-S6, decision 4): from the handler's comfortable
	  stop call to standstill, the contract's comfortable settle at the
	  entry speed (9996.67 ms at 8.33 m/s) plus its response (943 ms),
	  rounded up to the next ms. Not at standstill by then, the comfortable
	  stop has failed and the supervisor commands the emergency stop.

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_STANDSTILL_OBSERVE_MS
	int "How late the island sees a standstill (ms)"
	default 136
	help
	  Added to route 2's due: the island judges the standstill from its
	  latest kinematic_state take, which comes up to one sample period
	  (10 Hz, 100 ms) plus the serial link after the vehicle stopped. The
	  link is kinematic_state's own max_transport (36 ms, measured on the
	  board: 29.43 x 1.2; the contract names the runs). Without it a stop
	  inside the settle was judged late on the board (s6-b8-1: standstill
	  52 ms before the due, not yet seen on the island).

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_SWT
	bool "Feed SWT0 once per spin; its first timeout drives the same output"
	depends on WATCHDOG
	default y
	help
	  The S32K344's software watchdog (Zephyr wdt_nxp_s32, swt0, left
	  disabled at boot) is installed and started at the first spin, after
	  registration, and fed at the start of every spin. Its first timeout
	  raises an interrupt whose callback wakes the reaction thread: a hung
	  executor is caught even when no supervised route is pending. The
	  second consecutive timeout resets the MCU, so once the output is
	  latched the reaction thread feeds SWT0 itself: the reset is left for
	  a reaction thread that cannot run. Paused while a debugger halts the
	  core.

config ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_SWT_MS
	int "SWT0 timeout (ms)"
	depends on ISLAND_SUPERVISOR_SWT
	default 300
	help
	  Above the longest spin. island_supervisor_record.spin_gap_max_us is
	  the longest feed-to-feed gap seen since SWT0 started. The executor's
	  park ends at the next timer (33 ms, the emergency operator) and the
	  longest callback is the handler's tick (25.04 ms on the board,
	  phase9-W5).

config ISLAND_DEBUG_SPIN_STALL
	bool "Debug hook: stall the spin thread on command (test only)"
	help
	  Compiles in island_debug_spin_stall, a volatile u32 read at the
	  start of every spin. A debugger writing non-zero into it makes the
	  next spin busy-wait forever before feeding SWT0: the bench check of
	  the watchdog rung with no route pending (tools/board/
	  violations.py spin-stall). Inert while the word is 0, as it is
	  from boot: only a debugger writes it.

	  Also island_debug_late_return_ms (violations.py late-return N): the
	  next spin that answered the executor's supervised route (route 1)
	  before its due holds until N ms past that due before the supervisor
	  hears of it, once: the late notice of a route answered in time.

endif

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
